---
import Artwork from "@/components/Artwork.astro"
import CampaignPartners from "@/components/CampaignPartners.astro"
import CampaignStatus from "@/components/CampaignStatus.astro"
import LegalRoute from "@/components/LegalRoute.astro"
import MetaPage from "@/components/MetaPage.astro"
import StoryScene from "@/components/StoryScene.astro"
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro"
import { campaignStatus } from "@/data/campaign"
---

<Layout>
  <MetaPage slot="head" />
  <article class="campaign landing">
    <header class="hero">
      <CampaignStatus language="en" />
      <h1>
        Financial privacy is a fundamental right. <span>Let’s defend it.</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="lead">
        You use your money to pay for medical treatment, support relatives or
        donate to a political cause. That is part of your private life.
        Financial privacy protects your freedom to make those choices without
        others scrutinising your personal life.
      </p>
      <nav class="hero-actions" aria-label="Get started">
        <a class="button primary" href="/en/get-involved/#spenden"
          >Support the case</a
        >
        <a class="button" href="/en/dossier/">What DAC8 requires</a>
      </nav>
      <Artwork id="privacyExposed" language="en" size="hero" priority />
      <p class="prose hero-context">
        Since 2026, crypto-asset providers in Austria have had to prepare
        financial data on all their customers for the tax authority, without any
        specific suspicion. We believe this collection under DAC8 violates
        fundamental rights and are preparing a legal challenge.
      </p>
      <dl class="keyfigures" aria-label="Key facts">
        <div>
          <dt>
            <strong>Customers</strong>
            are covered, even if their data is not ultimately reported.
          </dt>
          <dd>All</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>
            <strong>Retention by the provider</strong>
            of records documenting checks and reporting of your data.
          </dt>
          <dd>10 <small>years</small></dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>
            <strong>First report</strong>
            to the tax authority, covering the 2026 reporting year.
          </dt>
          <dd><time datetime="2027-07-31">31 July 2027</time></dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </header>
    <section id="problem" aria-labelledby="problem-heading">
      <h2 id="problem-heading">What is at stake</h2>
      <p class="section-intro">
        Your private life deserves protection, including your finances.
        Information about your money can reveal details about your life. If it
        is shared, misunderstood or stolen, you bear the consequences.
      </p>
      <div class="story">
        <StoryScene
          art="accountToRecord"
          language="en"
          title="Your account becomes a file."
        >
          <p>
            You buy crypto-assets from an exchange or broker with reporting
            obligations in Austria. Since 2026, the provider has been preparing
            your identity and annual aggregates for the tax authority.
          </p>
        </StoryScene>
        <StoryScene
          art="massCapture"
          language="en"
          title="Everyone recorded. Without suspicion."
        >
          <p>
            Providers must prepare reporting data for all customers, including
            those who will not ultimately be reported. This requirement appears
            in Austrian law, not in the directive.
          </p>
        </StoryScene>
        <StoryScene
          art="reportAssets"
          language="en"
          title="What gets reported?"
          wide
          zoom
        >
          <p>
            Once a year: name, address, tax residence, tax identification number
            and date of birth, plus value in euros, units and number of
            transactions for each crypto-asset and transaction type. Wallet
            addresses, individual transactions and your current balance are not
            included.
          </p>
        </StoryScene>
        <StoryScene
          art="yearsOnFile"
          language="en"
          title="More about you. Every year."
        >
          <p>
            Annual reports reveal activity. Austrian law provides for reported
            crypto-asset data to be deleted after ten years, compared with five
            for corresponding bank-account data.
          </p>
        </StoryScene>
        <StoryScene
          art="dataHack"
          language="en"
          title="A data leak. Personal consequences."
        >
          <p>
            A provider breach can expose the link between your name and
            blockchain transactions. Once that link spreads, it is hard to take
            back.
          </p>
        </StoryScene>
        <StoryScene
          art="rankedPortrait"
          language="en"
          title="Ranked by wealth. Marked as a target."
        >
          <p>
            Total value and number of purchases give a rough estimate of how
            much money someone moves in crypto, whether or not the estimate is
            right. According to France’s Gendarmerie, criminals use leaked data
            from a crypto tax service to select victims.
          </p>
        </StoryScene>
      </div>
      <a class="text-link" href="/en/dossier/#gemeldet"
        >What is reported, and what is not</a
      >
    </section>
    <section id="weg" aria-labelledby="weg-heading">
      <h2 id="weg-heading">What we are doing</h2>
      <div class="art-split even">
        <div>
          <p class="section-intro">{campaignStatus.en.text}</p>
          <p class="prose">
            We believe Austria’s collection covering all customers violates
            fundamental rights and seek judicial review. Whether these specific
            obligations are proportionate is for the courts to decide.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Artwork id="collectionChallenge" language="en" />
      </div>
      <LegalRoute language="en" />
      <div class="precedent">
        <p class="precedent-year" aria-hidden="true">2014</p>
        <p class="prose">
          The campaign against the retention of telephone and internet data is a
          precedent for resistance: in 2014, the Court of Justice struck down
          the directive and Austria’s Constitutional Court struck down the
          national law. That success shows that even sweeping surveillance laws
          can be changed.
        </p>
      </div>
      <a class="text-link" href="/en/proceedings/">Where the case stands</a>
    </section>
    <section id="handeln" aria-labelledby="handeln-heading">
      <h2 id="handeln-heading">How you can help</h2>
      <div class="art-split even reverse">
        <ul class="facts action-facts stack">
          <li>
            <strong>Help fund the case</strong>
            <p>
              Your donation primarily helps cover the costs of the proceedings.
            </p>
            <a class="button primary" href="/en/get-involved/#spenden"
              >Donate</a
            >
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Follow the case</strong>
            <p>We publish news and next steps here and via RSS.</p>
            <a class="button" href="/en/proceedings/">Proceedings</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Share the campaign</strong>
            <p>Ready-made graphics for every step, in German and English.</p>
            <a class="button" href="/en/get-involved/#teilen">Share graphics</a>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <Artwork id="collectiveDefense" language="en" />
      </div>
    </section>
    <section id="vision" aria-labelledby="vision-heading">
      <h2 id="vision-heading">What we want to achieve</h2>
      <p class="statement">
        Privacy needs no justification.
        <span>State interference does.</span>
      </p>
      <p class="section-intro">
        A liberal democracy needs limits on state power. Collecting everyone’s
        financial data as a precaution crosses a red line for us.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        We oppose mass surveillance. Specific suspicions of crime should be
        investigated in a targeted way, with a legal basis and independent
        oversight. Uninvolved people must not be exposed to breaches, false
        suspicion and abuse through precautionary data collection.
      </p>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li>
          <strong>What we aim to achieve</strong>
          <p>
            Overturn disproportionate obligations, limit data collection and
            secure effective protection for those affected. We seek judicial
            scrutiny of the collection covering all customers, the reporting
            fields and the retention periods.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Why we are confident</strong>
          <p>
            We believe effective limits can be secured. That is why we combine
            concrete proceedings with political advocacy: in court and with the
            representatives who can change these laws.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
    <section id="position" aria-labelledby="position-heading">
      <h2 id="position-heading">Who we are</h2>
      <p class="section-intro">
        dac8.org is an initiative by Bitcoin Austria, the nonprofit Bitcoin
        association founded in Vienna in 2011. We are Bitcoiners and
        cypherpunks. We see privacy as a prerequisite for a free society.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        We stand for financial autonomy and against mass surveillance. The
        campaign concerns everyone covered by DAC8, regardless of which
        crypto-assets they use.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        Our campaign starts in Austria. DAC8 applies across the EU:
        organisations, businesses and experts who want to challenge the law in
        their country are welcome to contact us. We share arguments, sources and
        experience:
        <a href="mailto:kontakt@dac8.org">kontakt@dac8.org</a>
      </p>
      <CampaignPartners />
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
